==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|lang=ang|fangan}}, {{proto|Germanic|lang=ang|title=|fanhan}}, from an Indo-European root {{proto|Indo-European|lang=ang|title=|pang-}}, {{proto|Indo-European|lang=ang|title=|pank-}}. Cognate with Old Frisian [[fa|fā]], Old Saxon [[fahan]] (Dutch [[vangen]]), Old High German {{term|fahan|lang=goh}} (German {{term|fangen||catch|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|fá|lang=non}} (Danish and Swedish [[få]]), Gothic {{term|𐍆𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽||to catch|tr=fahan|lang=got}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin {{term|pango|pangō|fix|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/foːn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{Latinx|'''fōn'''}}

# to [[seize]], [[take]]

